### 10.2 Semi-gradient \(n\)-step Sarsa

We can obtain an \(n\)-step version of episodic semi-gradient Sarsa by using an \(n\)-step return as the update target in the semi-gradient Sarsa update equation (10.1). The \(n\)-step return immediately generalizes from its tabular form (7.4) to a function approximation form:

\[G_{t:t+n}\doteq R_{t+1}+\gamma R_{t+2}+\cdots+\gamma^{n-1}R_{t+n}+\gamma^{n} \hat{q}(S_{t+n},A_{t+n},\mathbf{w}_{t+n-1}),\hskip 14.226378ptt+n<T, \tag{10.4}\]

with \(G_{t:t+n}\doteq G_{t}\) if \(t+n\geq T\), as usual. The \(n\)-step update equation is

\[\mathbf{w}_{t+n}\doteq\mathbf{w}_{t+n-1}+\alpha\left[G_{t:t+n}-\hat{q}(S_{t},A_ {t},\mathbf{w}_{t+n-1})\right]\nabla\hat{q}(S_{t},A_{t},\mathbf{w}_{t+n-1}), \hskip 14.226378pt0\leq t<T. \tag{10.5}\]

